;;; An `(absent …)` over a pattern with a wildcard `_` always holds — M1e2
;;; **W20**, register row `W20/wild-in-absent` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; found by S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) probing the case's mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit z)` while there
;;; is no `holds` fact at all. `holds` is closed (`:no-hypothesis`), so no
;;; commitment extends it and the `absent` keeps the program monotone.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `_` in a rule's pattern matches any value, so `(absent (holds _))` asks
;;; whether there is no `holds` fact of one argument, and `(holds a)` is one:
;;; `z` never fires. The blind enumerator proposes `(hit a)`, so R is `∅` and
;;; `{(hit a)}`, no state holding `(hit z)`. It is the answer of
;;; `wild-in-absent-twin.ein`, this file with `_` written as `?any`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.20 `z` fired: `_` was an opaque slot that matched nothing,
;;; so the pattern under `absent` found no fact and the `absent` always held.
;;; A conclusion the program refutes was in every state: root held `(hit z)`,
;;; and R was `{(hit z)}` and `{(hit a), (hit z)}` (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `c73a0796`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same).
;;; Since then `_` matches any value, `z` never fires, and the program answers
;;; its twin's R.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule z () :match (absent (holds _)) :assert (hit z))
(solve :no-hypothesis (holds))
